# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-dribble — the alfe half of the dribble feature
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: feature-alfe-dribble

* Context

=dribble.issue= specified the feature for *both* clautolisp and alfe
(with whatever backend CAD). The clautolisp half shipped in 1.5.11 and
was verified end-to-end on 2026-08-11 (clautolisp 1.8.28); that ticket
is now closed. This one carries the alfe half, which is not started.

Verified 2026-08-11: =grep -rn dribble --include='*.lisp'
autolisp-front-end/= matches **zero** files. Nothing exists yet, so
this is greenfield rather than a gap-filling exercise.

* Problem

An alfe session driving AutoCAD or BricsCAD produces exactly the kind
of interaction transcript a dribble is for — arguably more valuable
than the clautolisp one, since a CAD session is harder to reproduce.
Today there is no way to record it.

** Scope, per pjb (2026-08-11)

"dribble sur alfe signifie que la fonction travaille avec bricscad et
autocad. alfe --clautolisp surement l'implemente deja dans clautolisp."

That is the right split, and it makes the two backend families
genuinely different pieces of work:

- =--clautolisp= backend: the engine already dribbles. The work is a
  *pass-through* — forward the flag into the spawned argv.
  =BUILD-SUBPROCESS-ARGV= (=autolisp-front-end/source/backend-clautolisp.lisp=,
  l.767) already does exactly this for =-Esource=, so there is a
  worked precedent to copy; today it forwards nothing dribble-related.
  For the *direct* (in-process) clautolisp variant the recording is
  already in-process and needs less still. One caveat: the spawned
  engine is launched with =--no-init= because "the front-end owns
  bootstrap/init policy", so the flag must come from alfe's own CLI,
  not from the user's rc.
- =bricscad= / =autocad= backends: nothing exists to forward to. These
  engines have no dribble of their own, so the recording has to be
  built on the alfe side from what alfe already sees of the
  conversation. This is the substance of the ticket.

* Proposal

Mirror the clautolisp half, whose implementation is the reference:
=clautolisp/tools/clautolisp/source/dribble.lisp= (tee/echo Gray
streams, write-time interactor filtering, O/E/C tagging), driven by the
shared CLI spec in =clautolisp/autolisp-cli=, which already carries the
option slots — =cli-options-dribble= and
=cli-options-dribble-interactors= exist and their comment already reads
"clautolisp today, alfe planned". So the parser work may be close to
zero; the work is the alfe-side wiring.

From =dribble.issue=, the alfe-specific requirements:

- CLI: =--dribble=, =--dribble=FILE=, =--dribble-interactors=IS= on the
  alfe CLI.
- Default path: =${XDG_STATE_HOME}/alfe/dribbles/${BACKEND}/YYYYMMDDTHHMMSS.log=
  where =${BACKEND}= is =clautolisp=, =bricscad= or =autocad= — note
  the per-backend subdirectory, which the clautolisp path has no
  counterpart for.
- Header: =;; H: alfe $alfe_version clautolisp|bricscad|autocad $cad_version=
  — two version numbers, unlike clautolisp's one. Getting
  =$cad_version= means asking the live backend, so decide what the
  header says when the backend has not answered yet (or at all).
- Same interaction-block format: raw input, then =;; O:= / =;; E:= /
  =;; C:= lines, same line-interleaving rule.
- Append to an existing file.

* Open question

What counts as an "interactor" on the alfe side, for the CAD backends.
The clautolisp filter keys off
=clautolisp.interactor:*interactor-stack*=, which a session driving
BricsCAD or AutoCAD does not have in the same shape. Either map alfe's
own phases onto interactor names, or declare =--dribble-interactors=
meaningful only for the clautolisp backend (where it is forwarded and
the engine applies it itself) and record everything for the CAD ones.

The second reading follows naturally from pjb's split above and is the
recommendation unless a use case for CAD-side filtering shows up.
Worth settling before writing code.

* Acceptance criteria

- =alfe --dribble= against each available backend produces a file at
  the documented per-backend path with the two-version =;; H:= header.
- Against =--clautolisp=, the flag reaches the spawned engine and the
  transcript is the engine's own (no double recording).
- =--dribble=FILE= writes FILE; an existing file is appended to.
- O/E/C tagging and the interleaving rule match the clautolisp output
  byte for byte for an equivalent session.
- Documented in the alfe user manual and RELEASE_NOTES.org.

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.124 / alfe 2.2.124 (2026-09-27)

Shipped. The open question above was settled the recommended way:
=--dribble-interactors= is meaningful only for the =--clautolisp=
backend, where it is *forwarded* and the engine applies its own
=*interactor-stack*= filter; the CAD backends have no such stack and
record everything. The flag is accepted in every case rather than
rejected, so a script does not have to know which backend it drives.

What was added:

- =autolisp-front-end/source/dribble.lisp= (new, package
  =alfe.dribble=): =start-dribble=, =stop-dribble=,
  =record-input=/=record-output=/=record-error-output=/=record-condition=,
  and =default-dribble-path=. The chunk recorder keeps =*open-tag*= /
  =*open-text*= so a partial line from one channel is continued rather
  than re-tagged, which is the interleaving rule the clautolisp half
  applies — the two transcripts read the same for an equivalent
  session.
- The header is =;; H: alfe <alfe-version> <backend> <cad-version>=
  with the backend name downcased. When the backend has not answered a
  version query yet, the third field is written as =?=: the header is
  emitted at =start-dribble= time, before the first round trip, and a
  transcript that names its backend but not yet its build is more
  useful than one that waits (or lies).
- Default path
  =${XDG_STATE_HOME}/alfe/dribbles/<backend>/YYYYMMDDTHHMMSS.log=,
  per-backend subdirectory as specified; =$XDG_STATE_HOME= falls back
  to =~/.local/state=. An existing file is appended to.
- CLI: =--dribble=, =--dribble FILE=, =--dribble-interactors IS= on
  alfe's own spec, in =--help= and in the man page.
- =--clautolisp --backend subprocess=: =build-subprocess-argv= forwards
  both flags, so the engine records its own richer REPL transcript and
  alfe records nothing (no double recording).
- =--clautolisp --backend direct= (the default): =(clal-dribble …)= is
  a documented no-op in-process, because clautolisp's dribble is a REPL
  tee owned by the clautolisp *program* and attached through an
  internal hook that alfe's in-process engine does not install. Rather
  than appear to record and produce nothing, alfe warns on
  =*error-output*= and names the two working answers (=--backend
  subprocess=, or run =clautolisp= directly). This is the one
  acceptance criterion deliberately not met as literally written, and
  the manual says so in the same words.
- =autolisp-front-end/tests/dribble-tests.lisp= (new): 14 tests over
  the header, the default path, append semantics, the O/E/C tagging and
  interleaving, the forwarded-flag argv, and the direct-mode warning.
- Documented in =autolisp-front-end/documentation/alfe-user-manual.org=
  (with the direct-mode limitation and its reason), the man page, and
  =RELEASE_NOTES.org=.
